WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.270
Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:05.340
I'm Yilun Du, a PhD student at
the MIT Computer Science and AI

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.280
Laboratory, or CSAIL,
and I'm researching

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.890
on how to construct
intelligent robot agents.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.965
Today, I'll be explaining
how AI image generators work.

00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:16.595
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.810
First question: how do large,
text-guided diffusion models

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:25.740
work?

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.690
So large, text-guided
diffusion models

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:31.290
are trained on a very large
data set of images and captions

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.340
on the internet.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.800
When you search up different
words on Google Search,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.570
you can find all types
of different images

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.890
for a particular phrase.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.660
Text image diffusion
models are trained

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.540
on millions and millions of
these image and text pairs

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.470
on the internet.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.100
And they're trained to be able
to reconstruct and generate

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.770
images that are similar to
the images on the internet.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.290
Because they're trained on
such massive amounts of data,

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:56.040
you can imagine that, when
you give this model a new text

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.510
prompt, because
it's been trained

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.330
to be able to recapture the
images that it's seen before,

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.599
it is able to generate an
image that matches the prompt.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:10.850
Next question: what makes
diffusion models so impactful?

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.905
So I think the main
reason why we're

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:15.280
seeing all these incredible
advances with these diffusion

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.850
models is because of the fact
that we are training them

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.620
with so much data and with
so much computational power.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.080
So the idea of
diffusion of models

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.540
themselves, it's
a very old idea.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.430
But what really
makes them work now

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:31.960
is the idea that we now have
so much computational resources

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.780
and so much computational power
that we're able to train them

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.785
at very large scales.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.210
Can you sell AI
image-generated images?

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.873
Yeah, I guess right now there
isn't really a copyright

00:01:44.873 --> 00:01:45.790
or anything like that.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.748
And I think people have
definitely been selling

00:01:47.748 --> 00:01:49.230
some of these AI artworks.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.360
I heard that
Midjourney Diffusion

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.940
was selling artwork for
millions of dollars each month.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.400
So yeah, I think you can
definitely sell them.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.233
I think this is a
question of like, maybe we

00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:00.233
should add some licensing
or something like that

00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:02.790
because these models are trained
on many, many images that

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:03.840
are copyrighted.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.250
But right now, it seems like
you can easily sell them.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.460
How are models prevented from
showing harmful or offensive

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.500
images, especially
considering the use

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.030
of open-source programs?

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:17.848
Yeah, this is a great question.

00:02:17.848 --> 00:02:19.390
In practice, these
models are trained

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.473
on hundreds of millions
of images on the internet.

00:02:21.473 --> 00:02:25.240
So there's all types of
harmful things on the internet.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.970
In practice, one way to prevent
these models from doing this

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.610
would be to very carefully
select and curate

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:32.638
the images that these
models are trained on.

00:02:32.638 --> 00:02:34.180
But currently, this
is a huge problem

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.555
with a lot of
these models, which

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:37.780
is why I think that a
lot of these companies

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.960
are not willing to release their
very large pre-trained models

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.340
for public use right now.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.740
Are there potential
applications of generative AI

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.120
outside of image or text?

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.150
Can you apply it, for example,
to robotics or control?

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.640
Yeah, I think this is a
really great question.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.440
Actually, I've been
working on some work

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.940
where we have been
directly applying

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.730
these generative models exactly
for the showcase of control.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.040
We have this paper called
"Planning with Diffusion"

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.110
where we can use
this similar process,

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.300
this similar generative
process to represent

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.350
different trajectories
of actions.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.460
So you can train these models on
huge amounts of robotic actions

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.368
or manipulation
demonstrations in video,

00:03:14.368 --> 00:03:15.910
and then you can
imagine you can give

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.630
the robot a new instruction
like, "Pick up this mug."

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:19.130
[WHIRRING]

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.510
And then it can use the
large model, generative model

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.270
that it's trained on millions of
other videos of robots picking

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.020
up objects.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.090
So I think there's many
different applications

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.060
for these generative models,
for example, in robotics,

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.780
or you can imagine,
for other domains,

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.710
such as protein synthesis
or molecular design.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:38.780
Well, it looks like we're
out of questions now.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.370
Thank you for
listening and asking

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.720
all these wonderful questions.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.630
Hopefully, you learned something
about AI image generation.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:45.700
Thank you.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

